For the CSC 317 project, we chose to develop an educational website about the environmental crisis currently impacting Australia’s Great Barrier Reef. We aimed to turn our idea into a website presenting real-world issues by using research data from Earth.org and indicators from Queensland Government State of the Environment report. The main objective of our webpage will be to deliver information about six important points on how climate change brought by humans is damaging the reef. We specifically focused on news based on paleoclimate studies that suggest Coral Sea temperatures reached their highest mark in 400 years and mass bleaching occurs regularly. By providing legitimate information in a simple way, we hope to demonstrate the importance of saving the ecosystem to our readers.

The intention behind page design was to represent it as a genuine article of environmental science instead of an elaborate website filled with many elements. It is a soft marine color scheme using deep ocean teal (#103c46) as the main color. The following color is the bright sea green (#168b95) that is a complementary color in the layout, while an off-white background will help to keep the text legible (#f4f8f7). In terms of typography, Georgia serif font was chosen for the header titles and section titles in order to grant an article-like style. Nonetheless, Open Sans font was used for easy reading and smooth text. The content was organized in chronological order starting with a basic header and a search bar, moving into the hero area that contains a reef image and the six climate facts in the form of individual cards.
